{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H8YGEWPkQJBTdjDVAXLnDZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VHJpwjCe9hDbG3aiQgrJos","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T015051/median/paper.tex","offset":196,"limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6056,"cache_read_input_tokens":46403,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6056},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9af548e6-918a-4fc8-985f-11ac7a31529f","uuid":"c58801ae-c637-4a69-a184-2506860ec5b0"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VHJpwjCe9hDbG3aiQgrJos","type":"tool_result","content":"196\tACR-VLA improves SmolVLA by $+6.0$\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (62.0\\%$\\to$68.0\\%), with consistent gains across all severity levels ($+6$\\,pp each at L3, L4, L5).\n197\tThe nominal LIBERO degradation is 2.0\\,pp (95.0\\%$\\to$93.0\\%), arising from occasional over-correction on in-distribution steps where conformal features show mild uncertainty.\n198\t\n199\t\\begin{table}[t]\n200\t\\centering\n201\t\\caption{Main results on LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus. SR = success rate (\\%).}\n202\t\\label{tab:main}\n203\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n204\t\\toprule\n205\tMethod & LIBERO & LP-L3 & LP-L4 & LP-L5 & LP-Avg \\\\\n206\t\\midrule\n207\tSmolVLA (base) & 95.0 & 72.0 & 60.0 & 54.0 & 62.0 \\\\\n208\t+ ACR-VLA (ours) & 93.0 & 78.0 & 66.0 & 60.0 & 68.0 \\\\\n209\t$\\Delta$ & $-$2.0 & +6.0 & +6.0 & +6.0 & +6.0 \\\\\n210\t\\midrule\n211\tSRPO & 97.0 & 87.0 & 81.0 & 78.3 & 82.1 \\\\\n212\tSRPO + ACR-VLA & 96.5 & 89.0 & 83.0 & 80.0 & 84.0 \\\\\n213\t$\\Delta$ & $-$0.5 & +2.0 & +2.0 & +1.7 & +1.9 \\\\\n214\t\\bottomrule\n215\t\\end{tabular}\n216\t\\end{table}\n217\t\n218\tWhen stacked on SRPO, ACR-VLA provides $+1.9$\\,pp (82.1\\%$\\to$84.0\\%), with minimal nominal degradation ($-0.5$\\,pp).\n219\tThis gain is modest and below the 2\\,pp threshold we set for strong complementarity evidence.\n220\tWe interpret this as \\emph{complementary in principle}: SRPO's training-time optimization captures most correctable error, and the residual gains from inference-time correction are diminished but present.\n221\tThe result demonstrates architectural compatibility---CRM can wrap any flow-matching VLA without interference---rather than a compelling empirical lift beyond training-time methods.\n222\t\n223\t\\paragraph{Inference Overhead.}\n224\tCGS adds 12.5\\,ms (10 flow-matching forward passes) and CRM adds 1.8\\,ms, totaling ${\\sim}8\\%$ pipeline overhead.\n225\tWith optional SnapFlow 1-step distillation, CGS time drops to 18\\,ms total (3.2$\\times$ speedup) at a cost of 1\\,pp SR.\n226\t\n227\t\\subsection{Ablation Study}\n228\t\n229\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} summarizes the ablation results.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{Ablation study on LIBERO-Plus (LP-Avg success rate, \\%). $\\Delta$ is relative to full ACR-VLA.}\n234\t\\label{tab:ablation}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lccr}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\tVariant & LIBERO & LP-Avg & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tACR-VLA (full) & 93.0 & 68.0 & --- \\\\\n240\t\\midrule\n241\tA1: Static calibration (no ACI) & 93.0 & 66.5 & $-$1.5 \\\\\n242\tA2: Sample selection only (no CRM) & 95.5 & 64.0 & $-$4.0 \\\\\n243\tA3: CRM w/o conformal features & 94.0 & 64.5 & $-$3.5 \\\\\n244\tA4: CRM w/ scalar uncertainty & 93.5 & 66.0 & $-$2.0 \\\\\n245\tA5: Bonferroni per-DOF & --- & 66.5 & $-$1.5 \\\\\n246\tA7: DiU aleatoric features & 94.5 & 65.0 & $-$3.0 \\\\\n247\tA7: Combined (52D) & 92.5 & 68.5 & +0.5 \\\\\n248\t\\midrule\n249\tSmolVLA (base) & 95.0 & 62.0 & $-$6.0 \\\\\n250\t\\bottomrule\n251\t\\end{tabular}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\t\\paragraph{A3: Conformal features as complementary signal.}\n255\tRemoving conformal features from CRM (A3) reduces LP-Avg by 3.5\\,pp (68.0\\%$\\to$64.5\\%).\n256\tThe A3 variant is equivalent to an observation-conditioned residual learner (similar to A2C2) and still achieves $+2.5$\\,pp over the base VLA, confirming that learned residual correction has value independent of uncertainty conditioning.\n257\tThe additional 3.5\\,pp from conformal features demonstrates that CGS-derived uncertainty statistics provide a complementary conditioning signal for CRM beyond what observation features alone supply.\n258\tThis gap clears our 2\\,pp significance threshold but is moderate, consistent with conformal features serving as auxiliary information rather than the primary correction driver.\n259\t\n260\t\\paragraph{A2: Learned correction vs.\\ sample selection.}\n261\tSample selection (A2) achieves only $+2.0$\\,pp over base, compared to CRM's $+6.0$\\,pp.\n262\tThe 4\\,pp gap demonstrates that CRM's ability to \\emph{synthesize} corrected actions---rather than merely select among existing samples---is the primary source of improvement.\n263\tWhen all $K$ samples are systematically biased under perturbation, selection cannot help; learned correction can.\n264\t\n265\t\\paragraph{A4: Structured vs.\\ scalar uncertainty.}\n266\tReplacing 45D per-DOF features with a scalar uncertainty summary (A4) reduces LP-Avg by 2.0\\,pp.\n267\tThe ablation ladder---no uncertainty ($+2.5$\\,pp), scalar ($+4.0$\\,pp), structured 45D ($+6.0$\\,pp) over base---shows that uncertainty features improve CRM regardless of representation, with structured features providing incremental value through per-DOF granularity.\n268\t\n269\t\\paragraph{A7: Epistemic vs.\\ aleatoric uncertainty.}\n270\tCGS epistemic features outperform DiU aleatoric features by 3.0\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (68.0\\% vs.\\ 65.0\\%).\n271\tUnder distribution shift, epistemic uncertainty rises as the model encounters unfamiliar observations, providing a stronger signal for CRM than aleatoric variance, which is present even in-distribution.\n272\tCombining both (52D) yields only $+0.5$\\,pp over epistemic-only, suggesting the aleatoric signal is largely subsumed.\n273\t\n274\t\\paragraph{A1: ACI contribution.}\n275\tACI with COC provides $+1.5$\\,pp over static calibration."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9af548e6-918a-4fc8-985f-11ac7a31529f","uuid":"141faf47-eb65-409e-897b-aa3d1f9ded41","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:15:19.178Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T015051/median/paper.tex","content":"ACR-VLA improves SmolVLA by $+6.0$\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (62.0\\%$\\to$68.0\\%), with consistent gains across all severity levels ($+6$\\,pp each at L3, L4, L5).\nThe nominal LIBERO degradation is 2.0\\,pp (95.0\\%$\\to$93.0\\%), arising from occasional over-correction on in-distribution steps where conformal features show mild uncertainty.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Main results on LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus. SR = success rate (\\%).}\n\\label{tab:main}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nMethod & LIBERO & LP-L3 & LP-L4 & LP-L5 & LP-Avg \\\\\n\\midrule\nSmolVLA (base) & 95.0 & 72.0 & 60.0 & 54.0 & 62.0 \\\\\n+ ACR-VLA (ours) & 93.0 & 78.0 & 66.0 & 60.0 & 68.0 \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & $-$2.0 & +6.0 & +6.0 & +6.0 & +6.0 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSRPO & 97.0 & 87.0 & 81.0 & 78.3 & 82.1 \\\\\nSRPO + ACR-VLA & 96.5 & 89.0 & 83.0 & 80.0 & 84.0 \\\\\n$\\Delta$ & $-$0.5 & +2.0 & +2.0 & +1.7 & +1.9 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWhen stacked on SRPO, ACR-VLA provides $+1.9$\\,pp (82.1\\%$\\to$84.0\\%), with minimal nominal degradation ($-0.5$\\,pp).\nThis gain is modest and below the 2\\,pp threshold we set for strong complementarity evidence.\nWe interpret this as \\emph{complementary in principle}: SRPO's training-time optimization captures most correctable error, and the residual gains from inference-time correction are diminished but present.\nThe result demonstrates architectural compatibility---CRM can wrap any flow-matching VLA without interference---rather than a compelling empirical lift beyond training-time methods.\n\n\\paragraph{Inference Overhead.}\nCGS adds 12.5\\,ms (10 flow-matching forward passes) and CRM adds 1.8\\,ms, totaling ${\\sim}8\\%$ pipeline overhead.\nWith optional SnapFlow 1-step distillation, CGS time drops to 18\\,ms total (3.2$\\times$ speedup) at a cost of 1\\,pp SR.\n\n\\subsection{Ablation Study}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:ablation} summarizes the ablation results.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ablation study on LIBERO-Plus (LP-Avg success rate, \\%). $\\Delta$ is relative to full ACR-VLA.}\n\\label{tab:ablation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccr}\n\\toprule\nVariant & LIBERO & LP-Avg & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nACR-VLA (full) & 93.0 & 68.0 & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nA1: Static calibration (no ACI) & 93.0 & 66.5 & $-$1.5 \\\\\nA2: Sample selection only (no CRM) & 95.5 & 64.0 & $-$4.0 \\\\\nA3: CRM w/o conformal features & 94.0 & 64.5 & $-$3.5 \\\\\nA4: CRM w/ scalar uncertainty & 93.5 & 66.0 & $-$2.0 \\\\\nA5: Bonferroni per-DOF & --- & 66.5 & $-$1.5 \\\\\nA7: DiU aleatoric features & 94.5 & 65.0 & $-$3.0 \\\\\nA7: Combined (52D) & 92.5 & 68.5 & +0.5 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSmolVLA (base) & 95.0 & 62.0 & $-$6.0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{A3: Conformal features as complementary signal.}\nRemoving conformal features from CRM (A3) reduces LP-Avg by 3.5\\,pp (68.0\\%$\\to$64.5\\%).\nThe A3 variant is equivalent to an observation-conditioned residual learner (similar to A2C2) and still achieves $+2.5$\\,pp over the base VLA, confirming that learned residual correction has value independent of uncertainty conditioning.\nThe additional 3.5\\,pp from conformal features demonstrates that CGS-derived uncertainty statistics provide a complementary conditioning signal for CRM beyond what observation features alone supply.\nThis gap clears our 2\\,pp significance threshold but is moderate, consistent with conformal features serving as auxiliary information rather than the primary correction driver.\n\n\\paragraph{A2: Learned correction vs.\\ sample selection.}\nSample selection (A2) achieves only $+2.0$\\,pp over base, compared to CRM's $+6.0$\\,pp.\nThe 4\\,pp gap demonstrates that CRM's ability to \\emph{synthesize} corrected actions---rather than merely select among existing samples---is the primary source of improvement.\nWhen all $K$ samples are systematically biased under perturbation, selection cannot help; learned correction can.\n\n\\paragraph{A4: Structured vs.\\ scalar uncertainty.}\nReplacing 45D per-DOF features with a scalar uncertainty summary (A4) reduces LP-Avg by 2.0\\,pp.\nThe ablation ladder---no uncertainty ($+2.5$\\,pp), scalar ($+4.0$\\,pp), structured 45D ($+6.0$\\,pp) over base---shows that uncertainty features improve CRM regardless of representation, with structured features providing incremental value through per-DOF granularity.\n\n\\paragraph{A7: Epistemic vs.\\ aleatoric uncertainty.}\nCGS epistemic features outperform DiU aleatoric features by 3.0\\,pp on LIBERO-Plus (68.0\\% vs.\\ 65.0\\%).\nUnder distribution shift, epistemic uncertainty rises as the model encounters unfamiliar observations, providing a stronger signal for CRM than aleatoric variance, which is present even in-distribution.\nCombining both (52D) yields only $+0.5$\\,pp over epistemic-only, suggesting the aleatoric signal is largely subsumed.\n\n\\paragraph{A1: ACI contribution.}\nACI with COC provides $+1.5$\\,pp over static calibration.","numLines":80,"startLine":196,"totalLines":413}}}
