\t\n312\t\\begin{table}[t]\n313\t\\centering\n314\t\\caption{Per-perturbation ACR-VLA gain (pp) on LIBERO-Plus.}\n315\t\\label{tab:perturb}\n316\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n317\t\\toprule\n318\tPerturbation & Base SR & ACR-VLA SR & $\\Delta$ & Augmented \\\\\n319\t\\midrule\n320\tCamera jitter & 58.0 & 67.0 & +9.0 & \\checkmark \\\\\n321\tObs.\\ noise & 55.0 & 65.0 & +10.0 & \\checkmark \\\\\n322\tRobot-state & 60.0 & 67.0 & +7.0 & partial \\\\\n323\tLighting & 65.0 & 70.0 & +5.0 & --- \\\\\n324\tBackground & 68.0 & 72.0 & +4.0 & --- \\\\\n325\tLayout & 62.0 & 64.0 & +2.0 & --- \\\\\n326\tLanguage & 66.0 & 67.0 & +1.0 & --- \\\\\n327\t\\midrule\n328\tAvg (augmented) & --- & --- & +8.7 & \\\\\n329\tAvg (non-augmented) & --- & --- & +2.3 & \\\\\n330\t\\bottomrule\n331\t\\end{tabular}\n332\t\\end{table}\n333\t\n334\tTable~\\ref{tab:perturb} reveals that ACR-VLA's gains concentrate on perturbation types covered by synthetic augmentation during CRM training.\n335\tCamera jitter ($+9$\\,pp) and observation noise ($+10$\\,pp) show the strongest improvements, while layout ($+2$\\,pp) and language ($+1$\\,pp) changes---which alter task semantics rather than inducing action-space distributional shift---show minimal benefit.\n336\tThis is an expected and honest limitation: ACR-VLA corrects actions when the policy's uncertainty is due to observation-level perturbation, not when the task itself has changed.\n337\tFor semantic perturbations, training-time methods that modify learned representations (SRPO, RobustVLA) remain more appropriate.\n338\t\n339\t\\subsection{Feature Importance Analysis}\n340\t\n341\t\\begin{table}[t]\n342\t\\centering\n343\t\\caption{CRM feature importance by gradient magnitude (top 10 of 45 features).}\n344\t\\label{tab:feature}\n345\t\\begin{tabular}{clcc}\n346\t\\toprule\n347\tRank & Feature & Grad.\\ Mag. & Category \\\\\n348\t\\midrule\n349\t1 & std(joint1) & 0.180 & Marginal \\\\\n350\t2 & std(joint4) & 0.150 & Marginal \\\\\n351\t3 & std(joint6) & 0.140 & Marginal \\\\\n352\t4 & ellipsoid\\_volume & 0.120 & Summary \\\\\n353\t5 & radius(joint1) & 0.090 & Marginal \\\\\n354\t6 & radius(joint4) & 0.075 & Marginal \\\\\n355\t7 & std(joint5) & 0.060 & Marginal \\\\\n356\t8 & cov(joint1,4) & 0.045 & Covariance \\\\\n357\t9 & radius(joint6) & 0.040 & Marginal \\\\"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9af548e6-918a-4fc8-985f-11ac7a31529f","uuid":"a707dfde-7a82-4426-b124-fbeeecd45af4","timestamp":"2026-05-13T02:15:51.655Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_8/projects/acr-vla-conformal-refinement/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260513T015051/median/paper.tex","content":"The modest gap reflects COC's noisy proxy nature ($\\rho{=}0.40$); ACI's value is more in the principled online recalibration guarantee than in raw empirical lift.\n\n\\subsection{COC Signal Analysis}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{COC signal quality and ACI adaptation metrics.}\n\\label{tab:coc}\n\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nSpearman $\\rho$ (COC, task success) & 0.40 ($p{=}.002$) \\\\\nCOC per-step binary accuracy & 62\\% \\\\\nACI convergence & ${\\sim}$120 episodes \\\\\nACI target / achieved coverage & 90\\% / 88$\\pm$4\\% \\\\\nEllipsoid volume narrowing (vs.\\ static) & 15\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nCOC-based ACI LP-Avg & 68.0\\% \\\\\nOracle (episode success) ACI LP-Avg & 69.5\\% \\\\\nSupervision-freedom cost & $-$1.5\\,pp \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:coc} characterizes COC as an ACI signal.\nCOC's per-step accuracy (62\\%) is limited---it measures temporal consistency of the policy, which correlates with but does not cause success.\nHowever, the slow EMA dynamics of ACI are tolerant of per-step noise: the statistically significant episode-level correlation ($\\rho{=}0.40$) is sufficient for ACI to converge within ${\\sim}120$ episodes and achieve 15\\% ellipsoid volume narrowing compared to static calibration.\n\nThe comparison with oracle episode-level success (A6) quantifies the \\emph{price of supervision-freedom}: 1.5\\,pp in SR and 3$\\times$ slower convergence.\nWe argue this is an acceptable trade-off, as COC enables ACI adaptation during deployment without any external reward signal---a property essential for autonomous operation.\n\n\\subsection{Per-Perturbation Analysis}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-perturbation ACR-VLA gain (pp) on LIBERO-Plus.}\n\\label{tab:perturb}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nPerturbation & Base SR & ACR-VLA SR & $\\Delta$ & Augmented \\\\\n\\midrule\nCamera jitter & 58.0 & 67.0 & +9.0 & \\checkmark \\\\\nObs.\\ noise & 55.0 & 65.0 & +10.0 & \\checkmark \\\\\nRobot-state & 60.0 & 67.0 & +7.0 & partial \\\\\nLighting & 65.0 & 70.0 & +5.0 & --- \\\\\nBackground & 68.0 & 72.0 & +4.0 & --- \\\\\nLayout & 62.0 & 64.0 & +2.0 & --- \\\\\nLanguage & 66.0 & 67.0 & +1.0 & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\nAvg (augmented) & --- & --- & +8.7 & \\\\\nAvg (non-augmented) & --- & --- & +2.3 & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:perturb} reveals that ACR-VLA's gains concentrate on perturbation types covered by synthetic augmentation during CRM training.\nCamera jitter ($+9$\\,pp) and observation noise ($+10$\\,pp) show the strongest improvements, while layout ($+2$\\,pp) and language ($+1$\\,pp) changes---which alter task semantics rather than inducing action-space distributional shift---show minimal benefit.\nThis is an expected and honest limitation: ACR-VLA corrects actions when the policy's uncertainty is due to observation-level perturbation, not when the task itself has changed.\nFor semantic perturbations, training-time methods that modify learned representations (SRPO, RobustVLA) remain more appropriate.\n\n\\subsection{Feature Importance Analysis}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{CRM feature importance by gradient magnitude (top 10 of 45 features).}\n\\label{tab:feature}\n\\begin{tabular}{clcc}\n\\toprule\nRank & Feature & Grad.\\ Mag. & Category \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & std(joint1) & 0.180 & Marginal \\\\\n2 & std(joint4) & 0.150 & Marginal \\\\\n3 & std(joint6) & 0.140 & Marginal \\\\\n4 & ellipsoid\\_volume & 0.120 & Summary \\\\\n5 & radius(joint1) & 0.090 & Marginal \\\\\n6 & radius(joint4) & 0.075 & Marginal \\\\\n7 & std(joint5) & 0.060 & Marginal \\\\\n8 & cov(joint1,4) & 0.045 & Covariance \\\\\n9 & radius(joint6) & 0.040 & Marginal \\\\","numLines":80,"startLine":278,"totalLines":415}}}
